course: dictionary
lesson: 5927
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Marshmallow is a noun, so it names a thing. The clues around it show which thing. «Toast marshmallows over the campfire» tells you it is a sweet, because people toast food, not plants.
explain: The first meaning is a soft, white or pink spongy sweet made from sugar and gelatine. The second is a plant called the marsh mallow, whose root was used long ago to make the sweet. Read the words around the word: «hot chocolate with marshmallows» is about the sweet, not a plant growing in a marsh.
explain: Context can also warn you about danger. «Burning marshmallow sticks to skin and burns badly.» The clue is burns, so a hot, sticky marshmallow can hurt you. Always let toasted marshmallows cool before you eat them, and keep sticks away from faces.
fact: Marshmallow is a noun, and its plural is marshmallows.
fact: «Marshmallow» means a soft, white or pink spongy sweet made from sugar and gelatine.
fact: The marsh mallow is a plant, and long ago its root was used to make the sweet.
fact: «Toast marshmallows» means cook them over a fire, and toasted marshmallows get very hot inside.
fact: Marshmallows are a sugary treat, so they are for sometimes, not every day.
exercise: «Toast marshmallows over the campfire, then let them cool.» What is a marshmallow in this sentence? | *A soft, white or pink spongy sweet | A marsh plant growing by a river | A kind of campfire | A hot drink
exercise: The marsh mallow is a plant. What was its root used for long ago? | Lighting a campfire | A cooking pan | Keeping hot chocolate warm | *Making the sweet marshmallow
exercise: «Burning marshmallow sticks to skin and burns badly.» What does this sentence teach us? | *Let toasted marshmallows cool and keep sticks away from faces | Burning marshmallows make a good snack | Marshmallows are always safe to eat straight from the fire | Sticks are for drinking hot chocolate
test: «Build a tower out of dry spaghetti and marshmallows with a partner.» What are the marshmallows used for in this sentence? | *As joints that help hold the tower together | To toast over a fire | To eat in a hot chocolate | As a plant to grow in a marsh
test: «Roast marshmallows with a long stick or fork, at the side of the flames.» Why stand at the side of the flames? | To cook the marshmallow faster in the middle | To make the marshmallow lighter | To keep the stick warm for later | *To stay safer and avoid catching fire
exam: «In a test like the marshmallow test, cuttlefish waited for a shrimp they liked.» What does marshmallow test mean? | A test about plants that grow in marshes | A test about how to cook over a campfire | A test where children eat as many sweets as they can | *A test about waiting for a better reward, as the cuttlefish did
